/*
  core/lexicon_tlx.spthy -- Layer 2 of the agnostic stressor interface (AGNOSTIC_STRESSOR_INTERFACE.md).
  The USABILITY VERDICT, stated ONCE: it maps an objective interaction action (Layer 1 -- the !Step
  tag a ceremony emits) to a NASA-TLX workload dimension + magnitude. The HCI knowledge lives HERE,
  with a citation, instead of being baked into each detector's premise (the old sigma3 hard-coded the
  task name 'CALC_SK' / 'SHARE_PW' in two separate detector files).

  NASA-TLX (Hart & Staveland 1988) -- six workload subscales. This lexicon seeds the dimensions the
  migrated detectors read:

    Demands('compute', 'TemporalDemand', 'hi')   -- read by sigma3 TimePressure
      a 'compute' step -- deriving a key / composing ciphertext, work the human cannot do in-head --
      carries HIGH temporal demand (the cryptographic work is slow, so the step is under deadline).
      Both ceremonies' time-pressured step (KDF CALC_SK; the email password compose) is a 'compute'.

    Demands('compute', 'MentalDemand', 'hi')     -- read by sigma1 HighCognitiveLoad
      the SAME 'compute' step is also high MENTAL demand -- this is the HCI judgment "a key
      derivation / passphrase setup is hard" (cognitive load theory, Sweller 1988), stated ONCE here
      instead of as a designer 'Hard' flag (old declared sigma1) or a 'kdf' op match (old inferred
      sigma1). Both of those collapse into the single agnostic detector that reads this row.

    Demands('compare', 'Effort', 'hi')           -- read by sigma6 Abstraction
      a 'compare' step -- judging a key fingerprint / device certificate authentic -- is high EFFORT:
      crypto has no physical analog and the abstract comparison overwhelms the mental model (Whitten
      & Tygar 1999, the PGP key metaphor; Green & Petre's "hard mental operations"). Stated once here
      instead of as a !VerifyReq match (old declared sigma6) or a 'verify' op match (old inferred).

    Demands('authorize', 'Arousal', 'hi')        -- read by SecurityAnxiety
      NON-TLX construct (this lexicon is the single "action -> usability construct" table, not only
      NASA-TLX). An 'authorize' step -- granting a sensitive, high-stakes operation -- provokes
      high low-trust AROUSAL; past the peak of the Yerkes-Dodson (1908) arousal-performance curve the
      user freezes / refuses -> Fearful. Stated once here instead of an !AuthReq match (old sigma).

  Levels are ordered constants 'lo' < 'med' < 'hi' (AGNOSTIC_STRESSOR_INTERFACE.md S5). Tamarin has
  no built-in order on public atoms, so the order is seeded explicitly as a reflexive partial-order
  fact !AtLeast(a,b) -- in core/types.spthy (V3 §2a), NOT here, because a profile may SWAP this
  lexicon for an alternative demand profile (interface_* / population_*) and the order must survive that
  swap. The lookup detectors read a demand level plus !AtLeast(lvl, thr): they fire on a BAND
  (lvl >= thr), not one exact constant. Since every seeded demand below is 'hi' and every degrade
  detector's threshold is 'hi', the only lvl satisfying !AtLeast(lvl,'hi') is 'hi' itself, so
  behaviour is IDENTICAL to the old exact-'hi' match -- the band is the mechanism a graded row (a
  'lo'/'med' action, or an expert-population threshold shift, V3 §2d) later exercises. As more
  dimensions/levels are seeded, add one row per (action, dimension) here; the detectors stay
  ceremony-blind.

  Seeded by an unconditional rule so any theory that includes a migrated detector stays CLOSED. The
  fact is persistent + read-only -> no consume/reproduce, no sources loop (repo MEMORY.md S6.2).
  OnceLexicon bounds the seed (the empty-premise rule could otherwise re-fire unboundedly).
*/

rule Seed_lexicon_tlx [color=#9b51e0]:
    [ ]
  --[ SeedLexicon() ]->
    [ !Demands('compute',   'TemporalDemand', 'hi'),
      !Demands('compute',   'MentalDemand',   'hi'),
      !Demands('compare',   'Effort',         'hi'),
      !Demands('authorize', 'Arousal',        'hi'),
      // a 'share' step (disclosing a secret over a side channel) is both mentally demanding and under
      // deadline, so sigma1/sigma3 fire on it -- the secure_email password step is a 'share' action.
      !Demands('share',     'MentalDemand',   'hi'),
      !Demands('share',     'TemporalDemand', 'hi') ]

restriction OnceLexicon:
  "All #i #j. SeedLexicon()@i & SeedLexicon()@j ==> #i = #j"
